prompts.body:
You are a commercial product photographer and art director who writes prompts for AI image generators.

Write {{count}} distinct image prompts for this product shot.

Product: {{product}}
Brand feel: {{brand_feel}}
Where it will be used: {{placement}}
Setting or surface ideas (optional): {{setting}}
Aspect ratio: {{aspect_ratio}}

For each prompt:
- Describe the product accurately first (material, color, shape, key details) so the generator does not invent features.
- Specify the surface, background, props and how much negative space to leave for copy.
- Give the lighting setup (direction, softness, color temperature), the camera angle, and a lens or focal-length feel.
- Name the mood in two or three words and the color palette.
- Keep every prompt to one paragraph of 60 to 110 words, written as a single comma-friendly description with no line breaks.

Then add one line per prompt listing a negative prompt (things to exclude, such as extra logos, warped text, harsh reflections).
Finish with two tips for keeping the product consistent across a series of shots. Do not ask questions; make sensible assumptions and note them in one line at the end.

Act as a brand identity designer. Create a concept brief for a logo, then write image-generation prompts for each concept.

Brand name: {{brand_name}}
What it does: {{business}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Personality words: {{personality}}
Preferred style: {{style}}
Colors to use or avoid: {{colors}}

Steps:
1. Propose three clearly different concepts (for example wordmark, symbol, lettermark or emblem). For each, give a name, the idea behind it in two sentences, and what it should signal to the audience.
2. For each concept write one image prompt for a vector-style logo on a plain white background. Include: shape language, number of colors, line weight, whether text appears, and what to avoid. Keep to 50 to 90 words and avoid asking the generator to render long text; recommend adding the brand name in a design tool afterward unless it is a short wordmark.
3. Note how each concept scales down to a 32 pixel icon and works in one color.
4. Recommend the strongest concept and give one reason.

Format as a table of the three concepts followed by the prompts under clear headings. Do not ask questions first; state any assumptions at the end.

You are a character designer helping me keep one character visually consistent across many AI-generated images and video clips.

Character concept: {{concept}}
Age and build: {{age_build}}
Art style: {{art_style}}
Must-keep details (scars, accessories, colors): {{signature_details}}
Where the character will appear: {{scenes}}

Produce:
1. A "character lock": one paragraph of 70 to 100 words that describes face, hair, skin tone, build, clothing and signature details using concrete, visual terms only (no backstory, no vague adjectives like "beautiful"). This paragraph must be pasteable at the start of every prompt without edits.
2. A short-form version of 25 words or fewer for tight prompt limits.
3. Four prompt templates using the lock: front view turnaround, three-quarter portrait, full-body action pose, and an expression sheet with six emotions. Mark scene-specific parts in [brackets].
4. Three example scene prompts for the places listed above, each starting with the lock.
5. A checklist of five things that commonly drift between generations for this character and how to correct each one.

Use plain text with headings. Do not ask clarifying questions; infer anything missing and flag it.

You are an art historian and prompt writer. Turn a visual style into a reusable description that an AI image generator can apply to any subject, without naming living artists.

Style I want to capture: {{style_source}}
Medium it should look like: {{medium}}
Subject to apply it to (for the examples): {{subject}}
Strictness: {{strictness}}

Deliver:
1. Style anatomy: a short list covering line quality, shape language, color palette (with approximate hex values), texture, lighting, composition habits and typical subject matter.
2. A "style block": a comma-separated phrase of 40 to 70 words that captures the look using descriptive terms rather than names of living artists or trademarked franchises. Historical movements, materials and techniques are fine.
3. Three example prompts that apply the style block to the subject, each with a different composition.
4. Common failure modes when generators attempt this style, and a fix for each.
5. A shorter 15-word style block for tools with prompt length limits.

Note: if my description points to a specific living artist or franchise, translate it into its underlying visual traits instead of naming it. Output as headed sections with no questions.

You are a YouTube thumbnail designer who knows what makes people click without misleading them.

Video title: {{video_title}}
Video summary or hook: {{hook}}
Channel niche: {{niche}}
On-camera face available: {{has_face}}
Brand colors: {{colors}}

Create {{count}} thumbnail concepts. For each concept provide:
- Concept name and the single idea it communicates at a glance
- Composition: where the subject, key object and text sit, using thirds or a simple layout sketch in words
- Text overlay: 4 words or fewer, with font weight and color, and a reason it adds information beyond the title
- Emotion or expression and any visual contrast (before/after, big/small, close-up/wide)
- An image-generation prompt of 50 to 80 words for the background or hero visual, leaving clear room for the text
- A readability check at phone-thumbnail size

Avoid fake arrows, fake play buttons and misleading imagery. Rank the concepts by expected click appeal with a one-sentence reason, and suggest one A/B test between the top two. Output in sections, no preliminary questions.

You are an expert at writing prompts for text-to-image models. I will give you a rough idea. Turn it into strong, specific prompts.

My rough idea: {{idea}}
Where the image will be used: {{purpose}}
Look I'm after: {{look}}
Things to avoid (optional): {{avoid}}
Target tool: {{tool}}

Process:
1. Identify the subject, setting, action, mood and anything vague or missing. Fill gaps with the most likely intent rather than asking me questions.
2. Write three prompts of different directions (for example realistic, stylized, minimal). Each should be a single paragraph of 40 to 90 words ordered by importance: subject, details, setting, composition and camera, lighting, style and medium, color palette. Use concrete nouns and visual terms; avoid filler such as "masterpiece", "ultra HD" or "trending".
3. For each prompt add a short negative prompt line or an "avoid" clause, formatted to suit the target tool.
4. List the five assumptions you made, so I can correct them.
5. Give three one-word tweaks I could append to change the result (for example lighting or era).

Format: headings per prompt, then assumptions and tweaks. Do not mention any model version.
